I had someone tell me once: "They'd count the police horses if they could."

&nbsp;

A major university's bowl game win over a rival conference team had a higher attendance than a famous baseball player's final game, for similar reasons, as the football roster and support staff is larger than baseball. I like to remind my local baseball fan friend this detail often.

Pretend you don't like a major university in the Midwest or its arch-rival. Their standing in the conference doesn't have any impact on your team. You're a fan of a smaller school in the Mountain West. It's the Saturday after Thanksgiving. You sit down with a bowl of turkey and get ready to watch one of the biggest games on the entire calendar. You don't want a game that seems determined from the beginning, as the results have shown lately.

&nbsp;

Now, granted, many of these games have come down to the wire... But this year especially wasn't very fun for me to watch. I was really expecting to watch an invigorated defense from the larger program really play a stellar game against the rival. What we got was "more of the same," which is a quote from a friend that's very into college football. I'm looking forward to the next time the larger program wins, because it will be a huge shot in the arm to the rivalry.
